<section class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs" aria-labelledby="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs-heading">
  <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__container">
    <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__header">
      <span class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__eyebrow">FAQs</span>
      <h2 id="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs-heading" class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <p class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__sub">Everything else people ask when choosing between Google Forms, Microsoft Forms, and Formester.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__list">
      <details class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__item">
        <summary class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__q">Which is cheaper, Google Forms or Microsoft Forms?</summary>
        <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__a">Neither. Both form tools are free with a consumer account and bundled into the paid suites, and the suites are priced identically at every comparable tier: $7 a user a month annual and $8.40 monthly, then $14 and $16.80, then $22 and $26.40. Price cannot decide this comparison.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__item">
        <summary class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__q">Is Google Forms really free?</summary>
        <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__a">Yes, with any Google account, and Google publishes no response cap for it. What is documented is a limit of 300 pieces of content per form, which counts questions, descriptions, images, and videos together, plus 75 sections.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__item">
        <summary class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__q">Does Google Forms have a 300 question limit?</summary>
        <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__a">No, and this is the most repeated myth about it. Google documents 300 pieces of content per form, which is not the same as 300 questions. Microsoft Forms is the product with a hard question cap, at 200 per form.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__item">
        <summary class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__q">Can Google Forms be used for HIPAA data?</summary>
        <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__a">Yes, with the right setup, contrary to what several AI answers currently claim. Google names Google Forms inside Drive in its HIPAA Included Functionality list, but an administrator has to review and accept a business associate agreement first, and PHI must not be used until they do.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__item">
        <summary class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__q">Is Microsoft Forms covered by a BAA?</summary>
        <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__a">Yes. Forms is named in Microsoft's commercial in-scope list, and the BAA is available by default through the Microsoft Online Services Data Protection Addendum for covered entities. Personal accounts are out of scope.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__item">
        <summary class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__q">Does either one have AI form creation?</summary>
        <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__a">Microsoft does, with a licence. Copilot in Forms requires a Microsoft 365 Copilot licence, listed at $21 a user a month. Google's form drafting is part of Workspace Experiments, a trusted-tester programme, and it cannot build multi-section forms or edit an existing one.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__item">
        <summary class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__q">Which has better conditional logic?</summary>
        <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__a">Neither is good. Google branches only at section level and only from multiple choice or dropdown questions. Microsoft branches per question but can only move forward. Neither documents answer piping or compound rules.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__item">
        <summary class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__q">Where does Formester fit?</summary>
        <div class="fmstr-cmp-gvm-faqs__a">As the option when the suite's form tool runs out. Formester branches on any field in any direction, takes Stripe and PayPal payments on the free plan, generates forms with AI without a licence add-on, and ships an MCP server on Business. It is not SOC 2 or HIPAA certified, so regulated work belongs in the suite you already have a BAA with.</div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
